# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""PegaProx QNetd helper: the one thing that touches the QNetd side of a QDevice.

PegaProx sets up corosync QDevices for its clusters and relays the certificates itself,
so no cluster node ever needs access to the QNetd host. On that host everything goes
through this script, with a fixed set of operations:

    pegaprox-qnetd-helper version
    pegaprox-qnetd-helper init
    pegaprox-qnetd-helper ca
    pegaprox-qnetd-helper sign --name <cluster>    stdin: {"crq_b64": "...", "replace": false}
    pegaprox-qnetd-helper status
    pegaprox-qnetd-helper uuid
    pegaprox-qnetd-helper firewall --name <cluster>
                                    stdin: {"action": "inspect|allow|remove", "sources": [...]}

Each prints exactly one JSON object on stdout and exits 0 (ok) or 1 (error). It runs as
root over SSH, as `pegaprox-qnetd-helper serve` for the PegaProx on this host, or in the
QNetd sidecar of the Docker setup. `serve` is what pegaprox-qnetd-helper@.service starts
for each connection of pegaprox-qnetd-helper.socket (packaging/qnetd/install.sh writes
both units): the connection is stdin and stdout, one JSON line in and one out, and only
root and the PegaProx service user are answered. The sidecar
(`pegaprox_multi_cluster.py qnetd run`) loads this file and serves the same lines on a
Unix socket of its own.

`status` names the connected clusters and their nodes, and the cluster names that have a
certificate on file here (names only): a setup sees before it starts that `sign` would
need "replace" for its name.

`firewall` opens the port of corosync-qnetd for the addresses the nodes of one cluster come
from (ufw or firewalld; the name of the cluster is the tag of its rules) and takes exactly
those rules out again. It never takes the port from the request, and never deletes a rule
it did not add itself.

The NSS database is the one corosync-qnetd-certutil makes: same directory, file names,
nicknames, serial file and certificate validity. A QNetd that the stock tools (or pvecm)
set up keeps its CA, and the stock tools keep working on a QNetd this script set up.

Standalone on purpose: python 3.6 or later, standard library only, nothing imported from
pegaprox. Installed root-owned at /usr/lib/pegaprox/qnetd/pegaprox-qnetd-helper.

MK Oct 2026
"""

